[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix. It holds three problems. All three arrive
under the same generic subject lines ("Date is wrong", "Wrong day on the invite"), so the subject rule
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) merged them into one ticket. Each one now has a ticket of its own, with its count.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first row. The other two keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day
  first, so day and month swap.
- **hint.** `booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says,
  and the form reads it day first. The date is wrong before the parser sees it.
- **one-day.** The date moves by one day, not by a swap: Tuesday becomes Monday, Lisbon and
  Toronto get different days for the same meeting. That looks like a time zone. The desk already
  noted these reports; until now they were only a footnote under the parser.

## How the reports were split

- Cause by tenant, not by intake tag: the tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, and for
  this ticket it is wrong often enough to matter. The rules are in `causes.ts` and `problems.ts`.
- The one-day reports come from the desk's notes, and their text agrees: none of them names a
  swapped day and month.
- The export repeats rows across pages. Each report counts once, by id.
- Counts follow the desk's own view of #4471: a report counts under the cause its tenant files it
  under. The one-day reports are therefore in the parser count as well as in their own row.

## For the desk

- Read the body before merging when the subject is generic, like "wrong date" or "date is wrong".
- One cause per ticket. The same symptom can have more than one cause.

